WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.340
Why do two test-cross classes overwhelm the other two?

2
00:00:04.360 --> 00:00:07.560
A blue chromosome and a gold partner pair in meiosis.

3
00:00:08.900 --> 00:00:10.860
Each carries the same marker positions.

4
00:00:12.240 --> 00:00:17.220
Their allele combinations begin intact, yet the offspring bins grow unevenly.

5
00:00:18.340 --> 00:00:21.460
The puzzle is what those bins reveal about gametes.

6
00:00:24.733 --> 00:00:29.073
With scorable traits, a test cross makes one parent's gametes visible.

7
00:00:30.413 --> 00:00:34.033
The heterozygote can contribute big A big B or big A little b.

8
00:00:35.213 --> 00:00:38.513
It can also contribute little a big B or little a little b.

9
00:00:39.653 --> 00:00:42.613
The tester always contributes recessive alleles.

10
00:00:43.773 --> 00:00:48.573
Each offspring therefore carries a readable copy of the heterozygote's gamete.

11
00:00:49.617 --> 00:00:52.977
Independent assortment would feed every bin equally.

12
00:00:54.297 --> 00:00:58.897
Instead, the inherited combinations repeatedly fill two bins faster.

13
00:01:00.197 --> 00:01:03.097
That excess is the signature of linkage in this cross.

14
00:01:04.617 --> 00:01:09.617
Linked means the loci share a chromosome closely enough for their association to

15
00:01:09.617 --> 00:01:11.017
appear in the scored progeny.

16
00:01:12.067 --> 00:01:15.627
First read the chromosomes the heterozygote received.

17
00:01:17.067 --> 00:01:20.047
Big A with big B is one inherited combination.

18
00:01:21.587 --> 00:01:23.327
Little a with little b is the other.

19
00:01:24.507 --> 00:01:27.467
Those are parental, even before counting offspring.

20
00:01:28.747 --> 00:01:30.947
The mixed combinations are recombinant.

21
00:01:32.267 --> 00:01:34.127
This inherited arrangement is phase.

22
00:01:35.107 --> 00:01:39.027
Class size can support that assignment, but phase defines it.

23
00:01:40.050 --> 00:01:45.150
Each homologue has already copied itself, so four chromatids lie side by side.

24
00:01:46.270 --> 00:01:50.470
A crossover joins matching positions on non-sister chromatids.

25
00:01:51.630 --> 00:01:56.130
When the exchange falls between the markers, the right-hand segments trade places.

26
00:01:57.410 --> 00:02:00.150
Two chromatids keep their inherited combinations.

27
00:02:01.510 --> 00:02:06.450
The exchanged chromatids carry new combinations, which feed the recombinant bins.

28
00:02:07.633 --> 00:02:12.613
Now slide the right marker farther away while crossover positions keep appearing

29
00:02:12.613 --> 00:02:13.733
along the chromatids.

30
00:02:14.973 --> 00:02:18.653
Which pair of bins should claim a larger share over a short interval?

31
00:02:19.933 --> 00:02:21.913
Hold the chromosome picture in your mind.

32
00:02:27.900 --> 00:02:29.640
The recombinant pair grows.

33
00:02:30.820 --> 00:02:35.580
A wider interval offers more places for a crossover to land between the markers.

34
00:02:36.960 --> 00:02:42.000
Over a short interval, a second crossover between the markers is rare, so few

35
00:02:42.000 --> 00:02:43.280
exchanges stay hidden.

36
00:02:44.520 --> 00:02:49.160
The recombinant share can therefore serve as an estimate of genetic map distance.

37
00:02:50.250 --> 00:02:54.490
Add both recombinant classes before dividing by all offspring.

38
00:02:55.910 --> 00:02:57.350
Write the result as a percentage.

39
00:02:58.650 --> 00:03:03.250
For a short interval, that percentage estimates centimorgans on the genetic map.

40
00:03:04.450 --> 00:03:06.530
It does not measure a fixed length of DNA.

41
00:03:08.050 --> 00:03:12.790
Different chromosome regions and different sexes can convert physical spacing

42
00:03:12.790 --> 00:03:13.790
differently.

43
00:03:14.383 --> 00:03:16.143
The workflow is now stable.

44
00:03:17.643 --> 00:03:22.583
Read the parents. Name the parental and recombinant classes from phase.

45
00:03:23.883 --> 00:03:27.163
Add the recombinant classes, then divide by the total.

46
00:03:28.463 --> 00:03:30.663
Only then use the short-interval ruler.

47
00:03:31.923 --> 00:03:35.923
The next question is what happens when crossovers can hide one another.
